WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:05.350
Let’s say you’re visiting Earth for the
first time.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:10.650
And the tour of the planet consists of being
dropped into random locations for about five

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.980
seconds before being whisked away to an entirely
different spot on the globe.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:21.029
If you knew nothing whatsoever about life
on Earth, how might you interpret what you

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:22.029
saw?

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:27.240
At first, these narrow glimpses of a vast
world would be confusing, and probably a little

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.240
bizarre.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.190
But over time, as the brief snapshots formed
a greater whole — you might be able to get

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.820
a sense of broader patterns of life here on
Earth.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:42.750
‘Exobiotica’ is a speculative biology
project by incredible artist Michael Beaudry

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:48.070
that offers similarly limited windows into
the sprawling alien biosphere of a planet

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.780
called Veteris.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:55.120
In the words of the artist, “Each composition
is a tip of an iceberg – a momentary and

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.960
fleeting glimpse into an entire ecosystem.”

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:04.309
And the lifeforms we can document are uniquely
alien — even by the standards of speculative

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:05.309
biology.

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:09.990
Yet the more art you observe, the more you
begin to understand the larger stories of

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.119
life upon Planet Veteris.

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:17.249
So, for this entry into the Archive, we’ll
shine a light on the secrets of each region

00:01:17.249 --> 00:01:20.610
— and uncover how the larger ecosystems
fit together.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:25.409
And as always, you can follow and support
the artist using the links in the description.

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:30.829
Now, let’s set off towards Planet Veteris,
and prepare for our first glimpse of this

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:35.280
world of mysteries.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.390
Planet Veteris lies beyond the outskirts of
a stunning nebula.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:43.310
The very name — ‘Veteris’ – gives
us some understanding of the nature of this

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.289
world, as it means ‘Ancient’ in Latin.

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:52.920
And indeed, this planet is quite old relative
to Earth — at almost eight-billion years.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.329
Since this is nearly twice the age of our
home planet, there’s no telling what life

00:01:57.329 --> 00:01:58.829
has emerged.

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:02.229
Time for a closer look…

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:08.209
Touching down on Veteris, our first glimpse
of the teeming surface is difficult to comprehend.

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:12.370
At a glance, it’s hard to tell which of
these organisms are plants, and which are

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:13.560
animals.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:19.040
On Earth, broad categories like plants, animals,
and fungi are useful in the classification

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.150
of lifeforms, and it’s rare that one would
mistake one for the other.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:28.799
But the lifeforms on Veteris resist such classifications
entirely, and many remain unnamed.

00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:35.019
The large, plodding walkers move like animals,
yet they can photosynthesize.

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:39.700
The flower-like lifeforms might look like
plants, but they can ‘walk’ on their spindly

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:40.700
legs.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.090
The long, rope-like organisms that carpet
the ground can’t walk, but do grow at a

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.430
rapid pace to mark their territory.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:52.829
Even the rock-like spheres are secretly alert,
employing an electro-chemical defense to keep

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:54.800
the creeping tendrils at bay.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.150
The end result is an ecosystem with extreme
biodiversity that is truly difficult for us

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.959
Earthlings to comprehend.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:06.860
But the more locations we visit, the more
we’ll understand.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:12.950
The twisting matrix of tendrils define a region
known as the Low Forest, and here we’ll

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:17.239
discover another unusual organism roaming
amidst the strands.

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:23.340
This is a Dacia — a generalist hunter that
walks upon three flexible hydraulic legs,

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.610
which are powered by pressured liquid.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:31.799
An organism powering their legs with hydraulics
might sound like pure sci-fi, yet surprisingly,

00:03:31.799 --> 00:03:37.099
many types of Arachnids actually use pressured
liquid to help power their spindly legs.

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:42.069
And since Veteris’s gravity is lesser than
Earth’s, we’ll find many organisms get

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:45.020
around on similar hydraulic tubes.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:50.500
Aside from its locomotive limbs, the Dacia
posses small grasping appendages to help it

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.620
apprehend prey and communicate with other
members of its species.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:04:00.050
For the Dacia is a fairly clever and naturally
curious lifeform… which can sometimes lead

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:02.150
it into trouble.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:06.760
For the most part, Dacias stick to the Low
Forest when searching for food.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:11.849
In our next snapshot, however, we can see
a Dacia standing at the threshold of a very

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:13.450
different kind of environment.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:19.239
An organic cave marks the entrance to the
otherwise sealed Glow Forest.

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:24.560
Encouraged by the prospect of shelter, the
Dacia considers entering the dark, yet inviting

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:25.560
refuge.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.830
But what will it find inside…

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:34.190
Entering another snapshot, we can see that
within the Glow Forest, a mysterious ecosystem

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.370
awaits.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.910
Since this region possesses such a thick canopy,
virtually no light reaches lifeforms that

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:42.350
dwell within.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:48.690
As a result, these organisms have evolved
a startling array of bioluminescent displays.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:53.310
On Earth, surreal pageants of bioluminescence
can be found throughout the depths of the

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.660
oceans.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:05:00.570
These remarkable spectacles of light are thought
to serve a variety of functions — from communicating,

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:03.680
to attracting prey, to warding off predators.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.590
And in the Glow Forest, the mysterious displays
are equally diverse.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:14.050
This particular organism possesses glowing
bands on its back, likely to attract a mate.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:19.220
The vibrant colors and slug-like locomotive
appendages of this lifeform call to mind certain

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.470
genera of marine gastropods.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:26.680
And with a sharp, stinger-like protrusion
on its front end, this doesn’t seem like

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.870
a particularly friendly customer…

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:36.030
Closer to the forest floor, vertical glowing
bulbs sprout from the ground in vast quantities.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:41.130
The vibrant light they’re emitting indicates
these bulbs are on the cusp of reproducing...

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.319
but they might never get the chance.

00:05:43.319 --> 00:05:49.880
For they are under siege by a slow-moving,
yet ever-hungry behemoth — a Laterna Pulmonis.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:54.980
Although a placid, rather unintelligent sort
of fellow, this organism is nonetheless a

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.720
serious threat to the glowing bulbs.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.850
Like its food source, the Laterna Pulmonis
possess purple biolumescence, which helps

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.300
it blend into the surrounding forest.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:10.770
So, unless a very sharp-eyed predator comes
along, the Laterna Pulmonis will likely continue

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.690
its snacking...

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:18.569
In the murky ponds on the floor of the Glow
Forest, we can glimpse a surprisingly diverse

00:06:18.569 --> 00:06:22.889
community of organisms that thrive in these
cloudy pools.

00:06:22.889 --> 00:06:28.970
A miniature forest of towering colonial microorganisms
dominates the landscape, feeding off of methane

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:32.389
gas released by decaying organic matter.

00:06:32.389 --> 00:06:37.550
Upon this bedrock of the food chain, a plethora
of creatures creep and crawl about their daily

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:38.580
lives.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:43.199
On our planet, similar dramas unfold in coastal
rock pools.

00:06:43.199 --> 00:06:48.789
And while these pools might seem peaceful,
they are actually battlegrounds for survival.

00:06:48.789 --> 00:06:53.659
Predators like the starfish are constantly
on the prowl — and they in turn are sometimes

00:06:53.659 --> 00:06:59.050
swallowed up by certain sea anemones, which
grow large enough to consume almost anything

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:00.050
in the pool.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:05.550
And on Veritis, a similarly ravenous goliath
terrorizes the murky ponds.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:12.220
In the distance, the pond’s top predator
rises over the towers — a shadowy leviathan

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.900
that devours everything in its path.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.479
Other creatures need to keep their distance.

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:24.890
Far from the dimness of the Glow Forest, our
next snapshot brings us to the vast plains.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:30.620
Here, we can see a group of spindly lifeforms
called the Magnavindix engaging in a very

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.479
intriguing behavior.

00:07:32.479 --> 00:07:38.020
These imposing creatures, which stand around
fifteen feet, or four meters in height, are

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:41.699
shepherds of sorts, keeping watch over their
unusual flock.

00:07:41.699 --> 00:07:46.470
They spend their days herding the placid,
defenseless Lumentus that are their main source

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:47.530
of food.

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:52.669
On Earth, several species of ants are known
to engage in a similar behavior.

00:07:52.669 --> 00:07:58.770
These ants are herding aphids — small rotund
insects that excrete a sugary liquid the ants

00:07:58.770 --> 00:07:59.770
feed on.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:05.130
In exchange, the ants go to great lengths
to care for their wards, protecting them from

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:10.199
predators, carrying them to new pasture lands
to feed, and even encouraging them to reproduce.

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:15.689
And the Magnavindix are similarly involved
in the lives of the Lumentus, playing the

00:08:15.689 --> 00:08:21.240
role of both defender and matchmaker, bringing
pairs of Lumentus together when their bio-lights

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.199
signal they’re ready to mate.

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:28.810
Indeed, this symbiosis has grown to the point
where the Lumentus have no conception of danger,

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:34.419
and are free to grow heavy and slow as they
graze upon the expanse of fibrous purple organisms

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:37.789
that dominate this land…

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:44.169
Looking through another window, on Veteris,
it seems symbiosis has emerged in other unexpected

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:45.360
formulations.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:51.040
This spherical lifeform is a Round-Walker,
and while it might not look particularly mobile,

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.980
it does, in fact, amble slowly on its long
spindly legs.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:01.170
At the moment, this particular Round Walker
is beleaguered by a flock of flat parasites

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:02.579
clinging to its surface.

00:09:02.579 --> 00:09:07.930
Yet the Round Walker isn’t bothered, for
its surface is tended to by a spiny little

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.829
fellow called a Guardian.

00:09:09.829 --> 00:09:14.680
Like their name suggests, Guardians are the
defenders of the Round-Walkers, as well as

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:21.050
their cleaning service — and in exchange,
the Round-Walkers serve as their living homes.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:26.290
This mutualistic symbiosis has converged on
a partnership similar to that of sea anemones

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:27.950
and clownfish.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:33.690
Certain anemones rely on clownfish heavily
— as clownfish both keep the anemone clean,

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:38.390
provide nutrients in the form of waste, and
scare off any would-be pillagers.

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:43.829
In return, the anemone’s tentacles provide
a safe home for the tiny clownfish.

00:09:43.829 --> 00:09:48.180
The Guardians and Round-Walkers are similarly
linked, to the point where you’ll never

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.660
find either species on their own.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:56.620
And so, the Round Walkers amble along, each
crowned by a spiny guardian defending their

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:59.600
small world.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:02.360
Not all organisms are so harmonious.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:08.740
Deep within a forested valley, a familiar
arms-race has emerged in some very unfamiliar

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:09.740
lifeforms.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:14.670
Here, a predatory creature called the Veloxos
follows its hunting path by the river.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:21.200
It’s a capable hunter, with three powerful
hydraulic legs and a harpoon-like proboscis,

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.350
which extends almost to the full length of
its body.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:29.260
And this Veloxos has spotted its prey — a
Dish-Faced Septaped that emerged from the

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:31.310
undergrowth for a drink.

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:35.880
The Septaped’s appearance helps it blend
in among inactive lifeforms on the forest

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:40.820
floor — much like how Earth’s Bargibant's
seahorses have evolved to almost perfectly

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:43.450
resemble the sessile stalks of coral.

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:49.510
But like the specialized seahorse, the Septaped’s
camouflage only works for specific environments,

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:53.540
and in this light, it’s dangerously exposed.

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:57.860
Moments from now, an intense chase will begin
between predator and prey.

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:02.959
No matter which species triumphs, the perpetual
theater of natural selection will continue

00:11:02.959 --> 00:11:06.380
all across the planet.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:10.200
Natural selection doesn’t just breed competition
on land, however.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:15.040
Our next glimpse shows us a scene below the
waves of this alien world.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:21.230
Here, the quick and nimble Seaswifts jet along
the current, sporting long, lateral spikes

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:23.840
to keep predators at bay.

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.550
Spikes are a common defense in the natural
world, with certain types of marine gastropods

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:32.180
having converged on similar lateral defenses.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:37.170
Near the spikey Seaswifts, the more vulnerable
Bubble Swimmers drift along.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:42.070
These filter-feeders use wave-like ripples
in their translucent outer membrane to propel

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:44.430
themselves slowly through the current.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:49.630
Both the Seaswifts and the Bubble Swimmers
are on their own in the vast oceans…

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:51.760
But other species have partnered up.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:57.230
The Glass Colligatio is a composite organism
— a creature composed of multiple species

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:00.980
that share critical biological processes between
them.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:07.060
In the Glass Colligatio’s case, the larger
swimmer provides mobility, while its multi-legged

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:10.220
symbiont feeds more effectively than it could
alone.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:15.790
As discussed before on this channel, numerous
organisms on Earth are also comprised of multiple

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:18.770
lifeforms working in tandem to survive.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:23.920
Yet curiously, interdependence between two
separate species appears far more common on

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:30.530
Veteris than on Earth — a trend that will
only strengthen the more lifeforms we observe.

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:35.390
Near the surface of this world’s oceans,
one can — on rare occasions — catch a

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.639
glimpse of a bizarre display.

00:12:37.639 --> 00:12:43.290
An entourage of deep-sea behemoths are making
a brief ascent to the surface, releasing gelatinous

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:48.279
strings of embryos here in the sunlight where
their young will have better visibility upon

00:12:48.279 --> 00:12:49.279
hatching.

00:12:49.279 --> 00:12:53.699
While a lifeform blindly releasing huge clouds
of their children into the wild might sound

00:12:53.699 --> 00:12:58.230
like a bad strategy, it mirrors that of many
lifeforms on Earth.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:02.790
In general, there are two main reproductive
strategies on our planet.

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:08.149
Organisms that are K-strategists produce fewer
young, but place a greater investment in each,

00:13:08.149 --> 00:13:11.700
ensuring a higher percentage of their children
reach adulthood.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:17.130
But there are also R-strategists — which
flood the ecosystem with progeny so that regardless

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:22.230
of how many don’t make it, at least some
are likely to survive long enough to reproduce.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:27.240
While the behemoths are clearly R-strategists
— their children won’t be completely on

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:28.240
their own.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:33.760
A swarm of intrigued lifeforms tail the behemoths,
and will become unwitting hosts for their

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:35.570
multitudinous offspring.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:41.339
The embryos will stick to and develop upon
the surface of these surrogate species, until

00:13:41.339 --> 00:13:43.850
they’re ready to hatch.

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:48.300
Luckily for the host species, however, they
won’t suffer any ill effects from their

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:50.880
temporary parasites…

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:56.130
In the murky blackness of the deep sea, many
creatures use bioluminescence similar to the

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:58.240
lifeforms of the Glow Forest.

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:03.589
The amusingly-named Gullible Vagari wander
the depths looking for the matching biolight

00:14:03.589 --> 00:14:06.199
of another member of its species.

00:14:06.199 --> 00:14:11.010
Described as a hopeless romantic, whether
the Vagari’s search ends in success or tragedy

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:12.880
is a matter of fate.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:16.730
For there is dangerous predator skulking in
the abyss.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:21.990
The Giant Allicio is an organism that has
evolved bioluminescent appendages that mimic

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:27.820
the light of the Vagari — its main food
source — and lure them into its jaws.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:33.290
This low-energy hunting method has resulted
in a cumbersome lifeform that isn’t particularly

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:34.690
hydrodynamic.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:41.580
On Earth, the lumpy Frogfish have a similarly
ungainly body plan due to a convergent lifestyle.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:46.889
Like the Giant Allicio, the Frogfish baits
its prey with a specialized lure on the front

00:14:46.889 --> 00:14:48.170
of its head.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:54.590
After all, when your food comes to, you don’t
have to be particularly mobile…

00:14:54.590 --> 00:15:00.709
Back on land, a lifeform known as The Sunworshipper
leads a placid and cautious existence high

00:15:00.709 --> 00:15:02.329
above the ground.

00:15:02.329 --> 00:15:07.540
Like many other mobile creatures on Veteris,
The Sunworshipper receives a portion of its

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:13.029
energy from photosynthesis - as evidenced
by the specially-adapted appendages radiating

00:15:13.029 --> 00:15:14.240
from its body.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.070
To stay safe, the sunworshipper has evolved
‘eyespots’ on its appendages — which

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:23.410
give predators the impression of a much more
formidable opponent.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:28.740
This sort of defensive mimicry is convergent
with various Earth prey animals, perhaps most

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:33.050
notably the eye-spots on the wings of certain
types of butterflies.

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:38.069
For a creature that wants to stay hidden,
the coloration of the Veteris might seem strange

00:15:38.069 --> 00:15:44.370
or even gaudy by our terrestrial standards,
but in its native habitat the bright red serves

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:46.060
as excellent camouflage.

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:52.180
And in a world as extreme as Veteris, it pays
to blend in…

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:56.320
Our second to last glimpse at Planet Veteris
is one of the most striking.

00:15:56.320 --> 00:16:01.870
Here, the ruler of a misty valley watches
as a competitor makes their approach.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:06.990
These lifeforms are Greater Confo, highly
territorial organisms that do battle with

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:10.230
as set of forward-facing hydraulic spears.

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:16.790
This disputed territory is a surreal landscape
of fluid filled branching grows called Camcenas,

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:18.959
a type of photosynthetic lifeform.

00:16:18.959 --> 00:16:23.079
And this biome has hidden dangers.

00:16:23.079 --> 00:16:28.889
The glowing orange gel that coats the forest
floor is both tasty and extremely sticky,

00:16:28.889 --> 00:16:33.950
designed to ensnare hungry lifeforms that
will later be consumed.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:39.300
A flock of Bicolor Micans have noticed this
accumulation, are flying en masse to take

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.029
advantage of it – carefully.

00:16:42.029 --> 00:16:49.240
For even a creature as large as the invading
Confo might become trapped if it’s not vigilant…

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:55.370
And in our last image, we can see a scene
where seasonal rains have blessed an arid

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:56.370
region.

00:16:56.370 --> 00:17:01.410
Here, Desert Runners — lifeforms with now
familiar tripodal body plans — congregate

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:02.610
for a drink.

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:08.890
But the allure of water has drawn in another
organism as well — the unparalleled Monolith.

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:14.010
These living towers send thick tendrils towards
the shallow pools to drink their fill.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:18.640
The monolith may only get the chance to drink
in this way two or three times a year, but

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:22.440
it’s enough to keep the structure alive
during drier periods.

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:27.510
And along the surface of the Monolith, glowing
biolights shimmer in the setting sun.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:32.850
These points of light are signals to the Nox
that soar nearby — fliers that root within

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:38.460
the Monolith during the day, and in return
provide the structure with discarded food.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:43.320
Each Nox can recognize the unique pattern
of biolights on their home monolith out of

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:44.930
the crowd of others.

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:50.220
This final example of symbiosis may have been
a surprise at the beginning of our journey,

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:57.680
but now, it’s just another familiar expression
of Planet Veteris’s broader patterns…

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:02.480
Like an alien visiting witnessing Earth’s
splendor in brief snapshots, our understanding

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:07.680
of Planet Veteris will always be somewhat
limited, but we’ve now observed enough moments

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:12.290
across the planet’s surface to understand
many of its fascinating systems.

00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:17.190
It’s been a unique viewing experience, and
there may be more to come.

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:23.010
According to the creator, the larger ‘Exobiotica’
project will eventually cover other planets

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:28.180
beyond the world of Veteris, that will harbor
entirely new forms of life.

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:32.850
But for now, if you enjoyed discovering the
secrets of Michael Beaudry’s incredible

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:38.260
compositions, you can once again follow and
support the artist to see more exciting illustrations

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:41.030
using the links in the description.

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.510
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:48.720
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:52.280
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:18:52.280 --> 00:19:00.700
See you in the next video.

